	doi = {10.1016/j.enconman.2026.121380},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105033443074?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {This study evaluates and optimizes a hybrid energy system combining solar thermal energy, biomass gasification, hydrogen generation, and atmospheric water harvesting. Concentrated solar power (CSP) systems integrated with phase change material (PCM)-based thermal storage provide continuous and reliable energy generation. A hybrid dynamic and steady-state simulation framework captures transient energy interactions among solar input, storage, and conversion units. Detailed 4E (energy, exergy, exergoeconomic, and environmental) analyses indicate that the solar gas turbine section accounts for approximately 57% of total exergy destruction, followed by the biomass conversion unit with 33%. Parametric analyses reveal that increasing the gasifier temperature from 900 K to 1350 K enhances overall exergy efficiency from 45.2% to 52.0% and decreases CO<sub>2</sub> emissions from 0.284 to 0.213 t/MWh. Likewise, varying the steam-to-biomass ratio (STBM) from 0.5 to 3 reduces the total cost rate by 63% but raises the hydrogen cost by 45%. The system was analyzed under four distinct climatic conditions—Bushehr, Bandar Abbas, Babol, and Chabahar—to evaluate its adaptability to different solar intensities, ambient temperatures, and humidity levels. Under base-case conditions representative of Bushehr’s solar profile, the system delivers 5051.9 kW of electricity, 0.1307 kg/s of hydrogen, and 240 L/h of freshwater, achieving energy and exergy efficiencies of 60.7% and 47.9%, respectively. To enhance computational efficiency, four artificial neural networks (ANNs) were employed as surrogate models within a multi-objective genetic algorithm optimization. Under Scenario A—targeting exergy efficiency, total cost rate, and unit electricity cost—optimal outcomes of 51.44%, 20.18 M$/year, and 0.051 $/kWh were obtained. Scenario B, focused on hydrogen cost reduction, yielded 49.67% exergy efficiency, 23.99 M$/year total cost rate, and 1.14 $/kg hydrogen cost. Copyright © 2026. P