	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105022436629?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Rural building renewals with integrating renewable energy systems hold significant importance for achieving carbon neutrality and coordinating urban-rural development. Over their lifecycles, buildings may undergo multiple renewals to enhance energy efficiency, with different timing and sequences due to financial conditions and remaining lifespans. This dynamic evolution causes energy consumption curves to change over time, continuously affecting the optimal configuration of renewable energy systems. Existing dynamic building renewal models have low time resolutions, introducing decision-making biases: they typically compute energy consumption, carbon emissions, and costs on annual data, overlooking the connections among hourly energy consumption, production, storage, and electricity price fluctuations. While hourly models are precise but computationally intensive, heuristic methods like genetic algorithms may miss global optima. This study, combining DTW-DBSCAN, proposes a clustering-based simplification method to efficiently globally search for the optimal renewable energy system capacities and building renovation strategy to achieve the minimum life-cycle cost and life-cycle zero-carbon emissions of rural settlements during the observation period (2021–2060), with hourly resolution, dynamic energy-saving targets, and renewal timings. Using Zhuxiaohui Village in Zhejiang, China, as a case study, results show that the optimal strategy reduces cost by 56.8 % and CO₂-equivalent emissions by 126.6 % relative to a baseline. Annual-based accounting underestimates costs by 45.8 %. The proposed method exhibits computation times comparable to surrogate models and 22.5 % less than genetic algorithms, yielding lifecycle costs 17.5 % and 9.4 % lower than those methods. The methodological framework can effectively support the planning for the dynamic renovation and deployment of renewable energies in rural communities. © 2025 Elsevier Ltd},
