	abstract = {Syria's prolonged electricity crisis, combined with pronounced regional variability in solar resources, necessitates site-specific assessment of large-scale renewable power options. This study presents a governorate-level techno-economic assessment of parabolic trough concentrated solar power (CSP) systems across fourteen Syrian governorates using Response Surface Methodology (RSM) as an analytical tool. A fixed, commercially representative parabolic trough CSP configuration is evaluated under location-dependent Direct Normal Irradiation (DNI) condition, while thermal energy storage (TES) duration is treated as an operational parameter to enable consistent comparison of system performance between regions. High-fidelity simulations are conducted using the Greenius platform, and second-order response surface models are developed to quantify the combined effects of DNI and TES on annual net electricity generation (Wₙₑₜ) and levelized cost of electricity (LCOE). The results reveal substantial spatial variation in CSP performance across Syria, with inland governorates demonstrating significantly higher energy yield and lower LCOE compared to coastal regions. Among all locations, Rif Dimashq exhibits the most favorable techno-economic performance, achieving up to 190 GWh of annual electricity generation with an LCOE of 0.1255 €/kWh under high-DNI conditions. The findings provide the first governorate-level, data-driven CSP assessment for Syria and offer practical insights to support regionally targeted renewable energy planning and post-conflict electricity sector recovery. © 2026 International Energy Initiative},
