	abstract = {The integration of phase change material (PCM) in metal hydride (MH) hydrogen storage reactors enables thermal self-equilibrium, eliminating the need for an external heat source. However, traditional sleeved MH reactors with PCM (TSMHR-PCM) suffer from low hydrogen storage efficiency due to their limited heat transfer area. To address this issue, this study proposed a sandwich MH-PCM reactor equipped with U-tubes (SWMHR-PCM-U). A three-dimensional heat and mass transfer model was established and hydrogen storage performances of TSMHR-PCM and SWMHR-PCM-U were comparatively analyzed. Additionally, by response surface methodology (RSM), the interactions among the factors of U-tube distribution, volume ratios of inner to outer layers of MH V<sub>m</sub><sup>′</sup>, and volume ratios of inner to outer layers of PCM V<sub>p</sub><sup>′</sup> were investigated. Results showed that applying the U-tube and sandwich structure significantly increases the hydrogen storage rate and enhances the temperature distribution's homogeneity. The time to reach 90 % hydrogen saturation t<sub>0.9</sub> for SWMHR-PCM-U is 687 s, representing an 89.95 % reduction compared to 6833 s for TSMHR-PCM. The RSM model identifies the optimal design parameter as number of U-tubes in the inner sandwich N = 6, V<sub>m</sub><sup>′</sup> = 0.3, and V<sub>p</sub><sup>′</sup> = 0.55, reducing t<sub>0.9</sub> by 29.25 % compared to the original configuration. The optimized MH-PCM reactor reduces energy consumption and enhances thermal stability, providing a compact, responsive solution for integration with intermittent renewable energy systems. © 2025 Elsevier Ltd},
