	abstract = {Renewable energy sources such as wind and solar power are developing rapidly. However, using of wind and solar power alone has limitations in resource utilisation. To maximize the utilisation of wind and solar resources, the design scheme of a novel coupled wind-solar energy system of the fish-tail blades coupled with flexible photovoltaic panels is proposed. A scaled-down prototype is also fabricated to verify the feasibility of the scheme, and the electrical performance of the wind turbine and flexible photovoltaic panel in a rotating state is investigated. The coupled structure is optimized and analyzed using the four-factor three-level response surface method. A response surface model is constructed to define the relationship between the optimization objective, the combined power, and the design parameters. By comparison, the combined power of the three-blade coupled wind-solar system optimized using response surface methodology is 276.4 W, which is 35.7 % higher than that of the initial two-blade coupled system. The power increase is mainly in solar, where the solar power is increased by 57 % and the wind power is decreased. © 2025 Elsevier Ltd},
