	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105021002355?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {The thermo-hydraulic performance of liquid metal helical-tube heat exchangers plays a critical role in the efficient cooling of advanced energy systems. However, their behavior under multi-parameter coupling conditions remains insufficiently understood. This study addresses this gap by experimentally investigating the flow and heat transfer characteristics of liquid gallium in helical tubes, thereby providing the experimental validation of a low-Prandtl-number turbulence model. Building on this validated model, response surface methodology combined with the NSGA-II multi-objective genetic algorithm is employed to conduct systematic multi-parameter optimization of liquid lithium’s thermo-hydraulic performance. Results reveal that the Nusselt number is highly sensitive to variations in helical pitch and curvature, with pronounced interaction effects that become especially significant at low flow rates, whereas pressure drop shows much weaker sensitivity. Quantitative analysis indicates that superior heat transfer is achieved when curvature exceeds 0.16 and pitch is below 100 mm, while performance deteriorates markedly when curvature falls below 0.06. Further optimization identifies the configuration of 80 mm pitch and 0.1667 curvature (minimum pitch and maximum curvature combination) as delivering the best overall thermo-hydraulic performance of liquid lithium across most operating conditions. Compared with the low-curvature condition (0.0556@0.65 m s<sup>−1</sup>), the Nusselt number increases by approximately 20 %, while the pressure drop decreases by about 36 %. This research clarifies the coupled multi-parameter influence mechanisms and establishes a quantitative design framework with optimization strategies, providing practical guidance for enhancing the reliability and efficiency of liquid metal cooling systems. © 2025 Elsevier Ltd.},
