	abstract = {Inaccurate wind forecasting results in inefficient power generation, energy supply and demand management, and energy grid stability. Physical and statistical models used for traditional forecasting approaches are often unable to account for the complex, nonlinear properties of wind speed and wind power generation. However, machine learning (ML) has been shown to have great potential for better wind energy forecasting thanks to the availability of vast amounts of good-quality datasets. The current study investigates how we can incorporate open-source data sets from UCI and Kaggle for wind energy forecasting, and what the contributions of various machine learning approaches, for example, deep learning, meta-models, and hybrid models are towards the accuracy.md. This study creates and compares several ML models (artificial neural networks (ANNs), long short-term memory (LSTM) networks, random forests (RF), and gradient boosting) that predict wind generation power based on historical wind speed, meteorological features, and power generation data. Feature engineering, and data preprocessing techniques are then applied to optimize model performance and hyperparameter tuning is used to validate and make predictions robust. We find that ML-based forecasting models yield better performance than traditional methods in terms of prediction errors and energy dispatch in real-time. The study emphasizes the power of open-source datasets to drive research and promote reproducibility. The explainable AI techniques and federated learning for decentralized and privacy-preserving wind energy forecasting will feature our future work. These findings will help advance the use of machine learning in renewables integration while facilitating more extensive and reliable usage of wind power through modern energy systems. © 2025 IEEE.},
