	abstract = {With the increasing involvement of social capital in the electricity market and advancements in microgrid technology, a competitive game pattern among multiple decision-making entities has emerged. To balance the interests of distribution network operators and microgrids, this paper proposes an energy management model based on a one-leader-multiple-followers game between operators and multiple microgrids. Additionally, a Stackelberg game equilibrium algorithm utilizing the Kriging meta model is introduced to address the energy management challenges of multiple microgrids. Firstly, we establish a microgrid model encompassing gas turbines, distributed wind turbines, and energy storage systems, along with an operational cost function for the virtual power plant. Secondly, we construct a benefit and pricing model for the distribution network operator. Next, the Kriging metamodel is utilized to approximate the microgrid energy management model, and it is integrated with the particle swarm optimization algorithm to produce high-quality sampling points. This approach enables rapid and precise determination of microgrid transaction electricity prices and output plans, steering clear of excessive invocations of the lower-level optimization model, reducing computational load, and enhancing solution efficiency. Finally, case simulations demonstrate that our proposed method effectively resolves the energy management issues for multiple microgrids. © 2025 IEEE.},
