	abstract = {Distributed energy systems based on methanol utilization are regarded as the key to efficient supply of electricity and hydrogen for end users. In this study, a novel high-temperature electrochemical hydrogen pump (HT-EHP) was introduced to enhance the efficiency of a tri-generation system (Tri-EHH) producing electricity, hydrogen, and heat from methanol. The system integrated a high-temperature proton exchange membrane fuel cell (HT-PEMFC) and a reformer, where reformed gas first generated electricity and heat in the HT-PEMFC, followed by high-purity hydrogen production (over 99.97 vol %) via HT-EHP. Process simulation was conducted using Python and UniSim Design, with a response surface method (RSM) based surrogate model for parameter analysis. Multi-objective optimization using NSGA-II balances net power, hydrogen production, and cost, with Pareto solutions selected via multi-attribute decision-making. A comprehensive 4E (energy, exergy, economy, environment) analysis revealed a 14.3 % improvement in exergy efficiency, with levelized costs of 92∼118 $/MW∙h for energy and 2.73∼4.33 $/kgH<sub>2</sub> for hydrogen. The system demonstrated a 1.4 years payback period and low lifecycle emissions (0.30∼0.44 kgCO<sub>2</sub>e/kW∙h and 4.85∼7.00 kgCO<sub>2</sub>e/kgH<sub>2</sub>), proving its economic and environmental viability for fuel cell-based distributed energy systems. © 2025 Hydrogen Energy Publications LLC},
