	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105032161092?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {This study investigates the use of Quantum Machine Learning (QML) for metamodeling the techno-economic stability of an islanded smart microgrid, which can also be applied to larger and more extensive grid systems. Using a physics-accurate simulator, we generated a small, many-shot dataset for the proposed microgrid architecture that is powered by a wave energy generator. To address the scalability challenges of both classical simulation and quantum computation, we propose a Quantum Many-shot Out-of-Context Learning (QM-OCL) pipeline. This pipeline uses Principal Component Analysis (PCA) for data dimensionality reduction, which was also shown to mitigate the barren plateau phenomenon in the optimization landscape of the Quantum Neural Network (QNN). The QNN was trained in a context-free manner, and its performance was benchmarked against classical models, including Shallow Neural Networks (NNs), Deep Neural Networks (DNNs), and Support Vector Classifiers (SVCs). QNN achieves an accuracy of 0.75 on this limited data, slightly outperforming most state-of-the-art classical models that achieve accuracies in the range of 0.70-0.77. While the approach currently shows some limitations in generalization, this is expected given that the QNN algorithm is still in its early stages of development. This work highlights the potential of QML for metamodeling complex, data-scarce systems like smart grids/microgrids and shows that dimensionality reduction can significantly improve the trainability of QNNs. Future work will focus on larger datasets and a quantitative analysis of barren plateau mitigation.  © 2025 IEEE.},
