	abstract = {In the context of energy transition, widespread electricity price incentives have intensified load fluctuations, demanding higher accuracy in short-term load forecasting. Existing methods often ignore heterogeneous user responses to price changes, limiting model adaptability. This paper proposes a short-term load forecasting method that integrates price elasticity coefficients to mine user response patterns. Our contributions are threefold: (1) clustering users into distinct groups via K-medoids based on historical load profiles; (2) quantifying the price sensitivity of each group using elasticity coefficients to reveal response patterns; (3) incorporating these coefficients into a Radial Basis Function Neural Network (RBFNN) and tailoring inputs by user group to enhance prediction accuracy and generalization. Experiments confirm that our approach effectively captures differentiated price responses and significantly improves forecasting performance, supporting demand-side management and resource optimization in power systems. © 2025 IEEE.},
