	abstract = {Integration of EVs to the power grid via V2G system is a crucial concern for improving grid stability and maximizing the economic benefits of EV integration. An efficient V2G scheduling is evaluated in this study by comparing two intelligent optimization techniques, i.e., Q learning, a reinforcement learning (RL) algorithm, and Genetic Algorithm (GA), an evolutionary computation approach. Q learning is very adaptable as it enables dynamic decision making, and is very well suited to real time energy management scenarios. It however requires a lot of training time and computational resources. On the other hand, GA is easier to implement but brings rapid convergence and is not responsive to the fluctuating grid conditions. In order to deal with these challenges, a hybrid strategy is proposed, in which GA is employed to solve the initial optimal selection problem and Q-learning to optimize selections during the course of operation. This integrated method improves the reliability and exploitation of V2G systems, which guarantees the optimal energy flow, the economic return, and the environmental sustainability. The proposed framework facilitates smarter, data-driven EV-grid interactions within a modern smart grid infrastructure.  © 2025 IEEE.},
