	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105010191302?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {France’s nuclear reactor fleet forms a substantial component of its electricity generation mix, making load-following operation of Nuclear Power Plants essential to balance supply and demand on the grid. This need for flexibility has grown with the increasing integration of intermittent renewable energy sources and the strategic decision to limit full-scale renewal of the existing nuclear fleet. In response, active research is directed toward optimizing load-following reactor control. This work addresses this challenge by solving a bi-objective unit-commitment problem, employing meta-models that approximate the physical constraints simulated by ALGO, a load-follow simulator based on APOLLO3<sup>®</sup> for the neutronics parts and on its own modeling of the G mode. These meta-models outperform the computation time of ALGO by up to 8 orders of magnitude while maintaining in the best case average relative errors below 2%. Such efficiency allows for a more comprehensive exploration of the solution space, enabling better estimation of the Pareto front of the multi-objective optimization. We recommend using a Histogram-based Gradient Boosting Regression Tree, using at least 53 hours of operational history as input for the best Pareto front estimation. This approach offers a practical solution for optimizing load-following operations across large reactor clusters based on technical criteria related to increasing safety and operational margins. © 2025 AMERICAN NUCLEAR SOCIETY, INCORPORATED, WESTMONT, ILLINOIS 60559},
