	abstract = {The integration of Battery Energy Storage into Microgrid (MG) operations has drawn significant research interest owing to its ability to maintain power quality, reliability, and balance between generation and demand. Hence, researchers have now shifted their focus on evaluating Battery Storage (BS) capacity commitment through Distributed Energy Resources scheduling using an effective Energy Management Strategy (EMS) in MG operation addressing the intermittency of Renewable Energy Sources (RES). However, further research is required to investigate the BS capacity commitment under different initial State of Charge (SOC) conditions and demand types. In light of this, the present study aims to assess the BS utilization under different operating conditions of MG while solving the proposed stochastic EMS problem. Also, the stochasticity of RES power output is predicted using a Machine Learning based Gaussian Process Regression approach, and uncertain energy demand modeling is done using Monte Carlo Simulation. Here, the influence of various load characteristics on battery operational activities and its capacity contribution is assessed. A framework to address the proposed problem is developed and solved using the 'Artificial Electric Field Algorithm (AEFA)'. Case studies are performed to determine the impact of different initial SOC levels on battery operation and its capacity commitment in MG operation. Results reveal that the energy contribution of BS is critically dependent on load characteristics and initial SOC level and the average battery contribution is found to be approximately 60% and 73% of its maximum capacity for electrical and thermal load respectively.  © 2024 IEEE.},
